NAME
       devd - the LikeOS device manager: lists disks and mounts USB drives

SYNOPSIS
       devd [-f] [-v]

DESCRIPTION
       devd keeps the list of disks, partitions and USB drives, with the file
       system on each (FAT32, ext4, exFAT, NTFS, ISO 9660, swap), its label
       and UUID, and where it is mounted.  It follows the kernel's device
       events (/dev/devevents); without them it looks at /sys/block every
       two seconds.

       Programs talk to it through the socket /var/run/devd/socket: the
       desktop's volume agent (likeos-volume-agent), the file manager, and
       devctl(1).  Any local user may list the devices and follow their
       changes.  Mounting, unmounting, ejecting and formatting are done for
       root and for the user logged in at the console, and only on removable
       USB drives that hold nothing the running system uses -- never on an
       internal disk and never on the drive LikeOS was started from.  devd
       never mounts anything on its own.

       A volume is mounted at /media/<user>/<label> (the UUID or the device
       name when it has no label), always nosuid and nodev.  FAT volumes
       belong to the user they are mounted for.  /media and /media/<user>
       belong to root and only root can write to them; the user may enter
       their own directory through an access ACL (or the directory is
       readable by everyone where no ACL can be set).  When a drive is
       pulled out, its mounts are detached and their directories removed.

       Formatting runs mkfs.fat(1) or mke2fs(1); a full format writes zeros
       over the whole volume first.

OPTIONS
       -f     Stay in the foreground and log to standard error.  This is how
              init(1) starts it, from /etc/inittab: respawn /usr/sbin/devd -f .

       -v     Log debugging messages as well.

FILES
       /var/run/devd/socket   the socket clients connect to
       /dev/devevents         the kernel's device events
       /media                 where USB drives are mounted

SEE ALSO
       devctl(1), mount(1), mkfs.fat(1), mke2fs(1), init(1)

